personally Mass Effect means a lot to me  like we say we when when we create  something we put it out it is like you  know putting your own child out there  right that feeling still exists I mean  eventually I'll move on to another IP  I'm sure eventually and I had the choice  even previous to this right but but for  now Mass Effect it's it's too special to  me and it's too important I think to to  RPG errors in the gaming community I  mean who wouldn't take that opportunity  to do that right  [Music]  there are a few franchises that have  been able to leave a footprint on the  culture of videogames as prominent as  Mass Effect has the original trilogy  reveled in meticulous details depicting  fascinating alien cultures political  strife and interspecies tensions with  complex and memorable characters driving  the narrative for a generation of gamers  like me  Mass Effect is our Star Trek it's our  Doctor Who our Star Wars but as with all  major fandoms Mass Effect is not without  its controversies the original trilogy  was built on choice and consequence it  promised players that each of their  actions however big or small would have  ramifications on the outcome of the  story and across three games spanning  over a hundred hours it lived up to that  promise right up until its final moments  in Mass Effect 3 as the curtains drew to  a close and players took the final steps  on their journey the game jettisoned the  characterization and storytelling  nuances that it defined the series for  some fans these final moments have  marked the legacy of the series as  strongly as everything Bioware achieved  in the hours preceding them but now with  Mass Effect Andromeda the studio is  leaving Commander Shepard and the Milky  Way behind for both the characters in  the game and the people making it  andromeda is about venturing into the  unfamiliar in hopes of finding a new  beginning in this two-part series  looking at the development of Mass  Effect Andromeda we traveled to PAX East  in Boston just days before the game's  launch where we talked to by hour about  the series and its newest entry in  episode 1 we're going to look at the  impact of the original trilogy and the  controversy surrounding its ending  before delving into the future Bioware  hopes to build for the series in Episode  two this is the story of Mass Effect  Andromeda  [Music]  [Music]  Mass Effect launched in 2007 a year  filled with landmark titles the  influence of which are still seen in the  games of today it was a year in which  Call of Duty 4 redefined first-person  shooters for the modern age bioshock  enraptured us with a thought-provoking  commentary on player agency and Nintendo  reminded us of the pure joy gaming can  inspire with Super Mario Galaxy  so yeah 2007 was a momentous year with  the sheer brilliance of these  blockbusters dwarfing all but the most  groundbreaking releases and amongst it  all there was one title that really  stood out from the crowd it was a game  that while not perfect showed immense  ambition it blurred genre lines by  marrying deep role-playing with  third-person shooting to create an  experience that felt steeped in history  but was also forward-thinking but beyond  that it delivered an intricate sci-fi  universe on a scale that had previously  only been seen in PC MMOs and it did so  with uncompromising attention to detail  the first Mass Effect laid the  foundations for Bioware's narrative  aspirations establishing alien races  exploring their cultures and  interweaving complex political tensions  between them it was a game that invited  players to connect with characters on a  human level to learn about their history  their hopes their fears and consider  them throughout the investment people  had in these characters was leveraged  for emotionally charged moments in which  decisions could decide the fate of a  friend or even the future of a species  Mass Effect promoted choice to ensure  players felt they weren't just  experiencing a story but were writing  their own well building on this scale  and characterization of this nuanced or  perhaps the most enduring part of its  legacy for me and thousands of others  each Mass Effect games stirred a  childlike wonder the type that pierces  through reality to make you believe in  Hogwarts the force or the plight of the  Browncoats and for the people working on  Andromeda many of which joined Bioware  as fans and are newcomers to developing  Mass Effect it was no different  so I bought mass one on launch day and  you know I had always wanted a a massive  space opera type RPG to play it changed  how I looked at sci-fi videogames the  the amount of inspiration that I drew  from being able to meet all these new a  lien for the first time in a galaxy I  think was was awesome that was the major  thing really feeling like you were  telling a human story but in this brand  new area of all these different species  I had never met before  he kind of evoked a lot of the feelings  I had with Star Wars and properties like  that before the scale was definitely  impressive like not just the physical  scale the amount of places you could go  although yes that was cool too but the  sense of like no this is a whole galaxy  you can actually explore that that has  been around awhile and like you have an  important part to play but this universe  exists without you it has its own  history and characters have new thing  going on and I like that even if you  remember the intro to Mass Effect 1 at  the beginning you're a big deal in your  own right but you're not you're not do  the Grand High king of everything like  no you're second someone else is captain  to the ship the beginning I thought that  was really cool so I was sick the  weekend I picked up anyone played it two  days picked up one disc put me2 in and  just kept going so it very quickly  consumed me the fascination with it and  it just it really changed the way I  viewed games also as a player you know  being like oh I'll try something new and  then realizing wow this is an experience  in games I'd never previously you know  engaged with probably the one of the  more powerful moments to me if I  remember correctly it was the moment  with Legion when he you could either  choose whether he would die or I think  it was tally would die and that to me  was gut-wrenching because both of those  characters I'd loved and I remember  playing it through the one time and then  I think Legion died and like no no no no  no no no no this is wrong and then go  back load the save again play it through  then tally died the next time I'm like I  can't do it  That moment when  you can actually explore the inside of  the world the kind of gate at the  beginning and suddenly you land with  your Clemson Megu and you go around but  it doesn't matter the execution doesn't  matter because it awakes something in  you about the sheer diversity and  discovering but you realize after when  you keep playing a trilogy that that  feeling of curiosity the world to  discover actually applies to people to  the other character and this is what my  cific to explore etc and and this is so  for me the definition of mass effect as  a personal level starts there that  little childhood sparkled that solar  lights in you and say oh I just want to  explore it and then you want to explore  reiationship and then you want to  explore something  [Music]  but the Mass Effect series is not  without its flaws and for some the  concluding chapter of the trilogy  brought the series to an unsatisfactory  close  players had poured themselves into the  series and charted a path for the  narrative that felt unique and personal  to them they made the universe their own  but in its final moments their impact  felt diminished Bioware faced a  difficult challenge to finish the  trilogy it needed to exert its own  authorship over a narrative that until  then had largely been guided by player  decisions and in doing so it had to wrap  up the trilogy in a way that accounted  for all those decisions arguably an  impossible task  Bioware solution was to offer three  resolutions that together provided an  end to the broader narrative but failed  to properly pay off the many  relationships players had cultivated  over the series the response to the  ending was resounding with aggrieved  fans letting Bioware know of their  displeasure through various grassroots  movements and social networks it was a  lot of pressure on us and we put a lot  of time and love into tying off those  story threads but you never know right  you never know what what we think is is  like oh that's that's gonna nail it  that's gonna be awesome everyone's gonna  love this some people might not and you  don't really know until you put the game  out as we've seen in the past you know  we generate a lot of passion of our  friends and ultimately you need to take  that as a reward I people care about  what you do but you need to accept that  passion goes both ways  it really felt like it ended like I said  you know had the ending you know gone  off a little bit more smoothly I think  we would have really personally  emotionally had that closure a bit more  but as far as saying goodbye to the  characters in the world I'd kind of  already done that I felt like I've done  that through the process of just putting  that out there and developing it right  oh we weren't happy we weren't happy  because this this was our baby as much  as it was anyone else's right wanting to  send it off and make it you know  the satisfying thing that people wanted  but as time went on and as things died  down a little bit and we look back and  we continued with some of the DLC in the  Citadel we're still very very proud of  it we're still very happy with what we  did and I think it set the stage for the  future because it it formalized for us  that Shepherd story was done and that we  told all we wanted to tell in that  universe so it kind of prompted us to  look forward and say ok well what's next  one aspect in light of the controversy  the studio released the extended cut DLC  to address some of the plot holes fans  raised as problematic and explore the  aftermath of the final decision further  but by this time fan sentiment had  solidified this outcry of disappointment  shifted the focus away from what Mass  Effect 3 and the series had achieved  across three games the discussion became  fixated on the broad strokes that  outlined the narrative instead of the  finer details that colored it to quote  GameSpot's review Mass Effect 3 was a  game about saving the galaxy but a  galaxy is just a thing an idea an  abstract a meaningless collection of  plutinos planets and pulsars Mass Effect  is actually about saving people and  there's a big difference there  that difference was buried under the  controversy and like it or not it's the  starting point for Andromeda six hundred  years after that fateful conversation  with the catalyst mass effect is moving  on from Shepherd beyond the Milky Way  and the characters and stories that were  contained within it for the people that  created and shepherded the original  trilogy to its conclusion Andromeda is a  second attempt at realizing ambitions  that fell to the wayside the first time  around the interesting thing is this is  that as that you know Mass Effect  Andromeda even though it's the fourth in  the series is really like I said I've  said it several times it's a new start  but it was a new start on a new engine  it's a new console cycle it's a new team  and so more so than any of the games  since Mass Effect one this one feels  like it's got all those things that we  wish we could have done because we were  really doing them for the first time but  what ended up happening is that living  up to Mass Effect does not make things  easier alright so yes it's well-defined  but also we wanted of course to you know  moving some Gemini bringing new races  and everything but it's the constant  reminder that Mass Effect means  something to the fans and as much as you  want to move that game forward if you  don't deliver what people expect in the  Mass Effect game it doesn't matter for  me as much as it's a a constant pressure  on us it's a good pressure that keeps us  going and that's where I take my energy  so so my experience with Mass Effect is  less about the game itself and more  about what it means for so many people  and how that translates into our fuel if  you're not right it's like if you go to  the pack show floor tomorrow you will  see tons of Mass Effect cosplay you will  see hundreds of people who care  desperately about the franchise most  other developers can't say that they  have them  magic like that built into their fanbase  so they kind of lift us up right it's  it's them who make the possible the  possibilities for the future it's not  you know uh sir  [Music]  while the old guard ensures that my soul  of mass effect is enshrined it's  Bioware's new blood that is attempting  to redefine its identity for a new  generation  [Music]  fans are at the heart of mass effect and  in Episode two of this series we'll be  talking to the developers that admired  the franchise from the outside but who  are now charting its future from within  [Music]  you  [Music]

